issue.body:
The issue is demonstrated by the following two examples.

**1:**

``` javascript
/* eslint-disable no-console */
function f (input) {
  const [{prop}, second] = input;
  console.log(prop, second);  // <--
}
f([{ prop: "a" }, "b"]);
```

**2:**

``` javascript
/* eslint-disable no-console */
function g (input) {
  const {first: [el], second} = input; // <--
  console.log(el, second); // <--
}
g({ first: ["a"], second: "b" });
```

These two examples compile and execute correctly using Babel.  However, ESlint will report a `block-scoped-var` warning on line 4 of the first example, and for both locations where `el` shows up in the second example.

Looking [here](https://github.com/eslint/eslint/blob/master/lib/rules/block-scoped-var.js#L26-L39), there is no case for where `parent.type === "ArrayPattern"` or `parent.type === "ObjectPattern"`.  I would have submitted a PR, however this check needs to be context sensitive: the immediate parent is either an `ArrayPattern` or `ObjectPattern` **and** the parent's parent is one or more levels of `ArrayPattern`/`ObjectPattern` (checking parent's parent's parent, and so on), ultimately terminating in `VariableDeclarator` or `FunctionExpression`.

That sort of check does not appear to be supported by `estraverse`.  So, considering the largish nature of a potential solution, I didn't want to make the attempt without first soliciting feedback.

Thanks!

**Edit:** May be related to #2253.  If identical, please feel free to close.  However, the discussion there led me to believe a separate problem was being addressed.

I am refactoring an Express app to es6 and tried to do this:

``` javascript
module.exports = {
  index: ({
    app,
    query: { limit = 10, page = 1, type },
    params: { param1, param2 }
  }, res, next) => {
     // some code
  },
};
```

It runs and compiles as expected using babel. However, eslint gives me a bunch of `block-scoped-var` errors:

```
"limit" used outside of binding context. (block-scoped-var)
etc.
```

Is that a bug or am I doing something wrong?

Also posted on Airbnb style guide repo as it might be a problem with their `.eslintrc` file. (https://github.com/airbnb/javascript/issues/434)

`v0.24.1`
